  "text": "### Step 1: Understanding the Problem\n\nWe need to find all positive integers \\( n \\) such that \\( n + 2 \\) divides the product \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\). In other words, \\( n + 2 \\) must be a divisor of \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\n### Step 2: Expressing the Condition Mathematically\n\nMathematically, this condition can be expressed as:\n\\[\nn + 2 \\mid 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9)\n\\]\nThis means that \\( n + 2 \\) must be a divisor of \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\n### Step 3: Simplifying the Expression\n\nLet’s denote \\( d = n + 2 \\). Then \\( n = d - 2 \\). Substituting \\( n = d - 2 \\) into the expression, we get:\n\\[\n3(d - 2 + 3)(d - 2)^2 + 9 = 3(d + 1)(d - 2)^2 + 9\n\\]\nHowever, this substitution might not directly help us find the divisors. Instead, let's consider the original condition again.\n\n### Step 4: Analyzing the Divisibility\n\nWe need \\( n + 2 \\) to divide \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\). Let’s consider the prime factorization of \\( n + 2 \\) and see how it relates to the factorization of \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\n### Step 5: Considering the Prime Factors\n\nLet’s denote \\( p = n + 2 \\). Then \\( n = p - 2 \\). We need \\( p \\) to divide \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\nSubstituting \\( n = p - 2 \\) into \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n\\[\n3(p - 2 + 3)((p - 2)^2 + 9) = 3(p + 1)((p - 2)^2 + 9)\n\\]\nExpanding \\( (p - 2)^2 + 9 \\):\n\\[\n(p - 2)^2 + 9 = p^2 - 4p + 4 + 9 = p^2 - 4p + 13\n\\]\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n3(p + 1)(p^2 - 4p + 13)\n\\]\nNow, \\( p \\) must divide \\( 3(p + 1)(p^2 - 4p + 13) \\). Since \\( p \\) divides \\( 3(p + 1)(p^2 - 4p + 13) \\), it must divide either \\( p + 1 \\) or \\( p^2 - 4p + 13 \\), or a combination of them.\n\n### Step 6: Considering the Cases\n\n**Case 1:** \\( p \\mid p + 1 \\)\n\nThis is only possible if \\( p = 1 \\), but \\( p = n + 2 \\geq 2 \\) for \\( n \\geq 0 \\). So, this case is not possible.\n\n**Case 2:** \\( p \\mid p^2 - 4p + 13 \\)\n\nThis means that \\( p^2 - 4p + 13 \\) must be divisible by \\( p \\). Dividing \\( p^2 - 4p + 13 \\) by \\( p \\):\n\\[\np^2 - 4p + 13 = p(p - 4) + 13\n\\]\nFor \\( p \\) to divide \\( p^2 - 4p + 13 \\), \\( p \\) must divide 13. Since \\( p \\geq 2 \\), the only possibility is \\( p = 13 \\).\n\n### Step 7: Finding \\( n \\) When \\( p = 13 \\)\n\nIf \\( p = 13 \\), then:\n\\[\nn + 2 = 13 \\implies n = 11\n\\]\nWe need to verify that \\( n = 11 \\) satisfies the original condition.\n\n### Step 8: Verification\n\nSubstitute \\( n = 11 \\) into \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n\\[\n3(11 + 3)(11^2 + 9) = 3 \\times 14 \\times (121 + 9) = 3 \\times 14 \\times 130 = 5460\n\\]\nNow, check if \\( n + 2 = 13 \\) divides 5460:\n\\[\n5460 \\div 13 = 420\n\\]\nSince 420 is an integer, \\( n = 11 \\) satisfies the condition.\n\n### Step 9: Checking for Other Possible Values\n\nWe considered \\( p = 13 \\), but are there other possible values of \\( p \\)? Let’s check for \\( p = 1 \\), \\( p = 2 \\), etc.\n\n- For \\( p = 1 \\):\n  \\[\n  n + 2 = 1 \\implies n = -1\n  \\]\n  But \\( n \\) must be a positive integer, so \\( p = 1 \\) is not valid.\n\n- For \\( p = 2 \\):\n  \\[\n  n + 2 = 2 \\implies n = 0\n  \\]\n  Again, \\( n \\) must be a positive integer, so \\( p = 2 \\) is not valid.\n\n- For \\( p = 3 \\):\n  \\[\n  n + 2 = 3 \\implies n = 1\n  \\]\n  Substitute \\( n = 1 \\) into \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n  \\[\n  3(1 + 3)(1^2 + 9) = 3 \\times 4 \\times 10 = 120\n  \\]\n  Check if \\( n + 2 = 3 \\) divides 120:\n  \\[\n  120 \\div 3 = 40\n  \\]\n  So, \\( n = 1 \\) satisfies the condition.\n\n- For \\( p = 4 \\):\n  \\[\n  n + 2 = 4 \\implies n = 2\n  \\]\n  Substitute \\( n = 2 \\) into \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n  \\[\n  3(2 + 3)(2^2 + 9) = 3 \\times 5 \\times 13 = 195\n  \\]\n  Check if \\( n + 2 = 4 \\) divides 195:\n  \\[\n  195 \\div 4 = 48.75\n  \\]\n  Since 48.75 is not an integer, \\( n = 2 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n- For \\( p = 5 \\):\n  \\[\n  n + 2 = 5 \\implies n = 3\n  \\]\n  Substitute \\( n = 3 \\) into \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n  \\[\n  3(3 + 3)(3^2 + 9) = 3 \\times 6 \\times 18 = 324\n  \\]\n  Check if \\( n + 2 = 5 \\) divides 324:\n  \\[\n  324 \\div 5 = 64.8\n  \\]\n  Since 64.8 is not an integer, \\( n = 3 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n- For \\( p = 6 \\):\n  \\[\n  n + 2 = 6 \\implies n = 4\n  \\]\n  Substitute \\( n = 4 \\) into \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n  \\[\n  3(4 + 3)(4^2 + 9) = 3 \\times 7 \\times 25 = 525\n  \\]\n  Check if \\( n + 2 = 6 \\) divides 525:\n  \\[\n  525 \\div 6 = 87.5\n  \\]\n  Since 87.5 is not an integer, \\( n = 4 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n- For \\( p = 7 \\):\n  \\[\n  n + 2 = 7 \\implies n = 5\n  \\]\n  Substitute \\( n = 5 \\) into \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n  \\[\n  3(5 + 3)(5^2 + 9) = 3 \\times 8 \\times 34 = 816\n  \\]\n  Check if \\( n + 2 = 7 \\) divides 816:\n  \\[\n  816 \\div 7 = 116.571...\n  \\]\n  Since 116.571... is not an integer, \\( n = 5 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n- For \\( p = 8 \\):\n  \\[\n  n + 2 = 8 \\implies n = 6\n  \\]\n  Substitute \\( n = 6 \\) into \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n  \\[\n  3(6 + 3)(6^2 + 9) = 3 \\times 9 \\times 45 = 1215\n  \\]\n  Check if \\( n + 2 = 8 \\) divides 1215:\n  \\[\n  1215 \\div 8 = 151.875\n  \\]\n  Since 151.875 is not an integer, \\( n = 6 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n- For \\( p = 9 \\):\n  \\[\n  n + 2 = 9 \\implies n = 7\n  \\]\n  Substitute \\( n = 7 \\) into \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n  \\[\n  3(7 + 3)(7^2 + 9) = 3 \\times 10 \\times 58 = 1740\n  \\]\n  Check if \\( n + 2 = 9 \\) divides 1740:\n  \\[\n  1740 \\div 9 = 193.\\overline{3}\n  \\]\n  Since 193.\\overline{3} is not an integer, \\( n = 7 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\n- For \\( p = 10 \\):\n  \\[\n  n + 2 = 10 \\implies n = 8\n  \\]\n  Substitute \\( n = 8 \\) into \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\):\n  \\[\n  3(8 + 3)(8^2 + 9) = 3 \\times 11 \\times 73 = 2319\n  \\]\n  Check if \\( n + 2 = 10 \\) divides 2319:\n  \\[\n  2319 \\div 10 = 231.9\n  \\]\n  Since 231.9 is not an integer, \\( n = 8 \\) does not satisfy the condition.\n\nFrom the above calculations, the only positive integers \\( n \\) that satisfy the condition are \\( n = 1 \\) and \\( n = 11 \\).\n\n### Step 10: Calculating the Sum\n\nFinally, we need to find the sum of all such \\( n \\):\n\\[\n1 + 11 = 12\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]",
